{"body":"## Normative status

This is the repository-owned implementation gap and child proposal for `roccho-dev/adrs#211`.

It introduces no new parent architecture. `adrs#211` remains the single decision and closure authority for JSONL-only local tools, agent lifecycle, canonical results, and reconstructible Herdr-backed run views.

Opening this issue authorizes no schema expansion by itself. Before implementation changes the merged `hq.local-tool.v1` wire or lifecycle behavior, `adrs#211` must record one latest-writer checkpoint fixing the smallest exact lifecycle, binding-owned environment, and native-reference contract required by the physical Herdr proof.

Existing accepted boundaries remain unchanged:

- `adrs#220`: finite Vim-to-bin V1; no generic PTY, arbitrary interactive shell, generic SSH executor, or generic OCI executor;
- `adrs#222`: verified-resource invocation remains non-interactive direct execution and excludes PTY/TUI, interactive authentication, generic SSH, and generic OCI;
- `adrs#226` / `envs#50` / `envs#51`: Windows physical ownership of the managed worker only; no command or adapter semantics;
- `hq#115`: two explicit-location remote-agent E2E proofs through existing adapters; not a generic runtime migration lane.

`adrs#221` is unrelated CI/coordination policy and is not a dependency.

## Goal

Reach the `adrs#211` target in which Herdr is one replaceable JSONL-defined local tool rather than a privileged production target in hq core, worker core, LSP, or Vimscript.

```text
append-only world definition and exact release
  -> hq.local-tool.v1 Herdr tool/action meaning
  -> exact envs/profile binding_ref
  -> immutable selected hq.world.v1
  -> already-running managed hq-worker
  -> generic local-tool preparation and bounded direct argv
  -> exact verified Herdr executable/API
  -> Herdr-owned native process/pane
  -> canonical result.v1 + non-authoritative native references
  -> finite read/observe/reconstruct operations without process replay
```

After completion, adding a later supported Herdr action requires only append-only world/release data and exact binding/profile data. It requires zero new hq generic-core branch, zero new managed-worker target registration, and zero Vimscript provider meaning.

The first physical consumer is the existing real-Vim `oci.aws.login.herdr` journey: one explicit submit starts the exact OCI AWS login argv in a visible Herdr pane and returns canonical evidence and native pane/process references without a dedicated `target=herdr` production dependency.

## Current observed gap

Physical Windows evidence on 2026-07-16:

- installed official hq/hq-worker revision: `3118886f34ac5615e8a7732a6297bd41900e21e1`;
- current hq `proposals`: `24ce4e8997875c60122217374137e917404ea203`;
- managed worker claim: `claim-0ba0d834d3b1ef9c428166e57ff0d209`;
- visible Herdr-owned worker: `hq-worker-commentsdev`, pane `wN:p3`, PID `22556`;
- submitted instruction: `ins-lsp-07bbe02985c06089c868320551bc882b`;
- submitted command: `oci.aws.login.herdr`;
- terminal result: `adapter_unavailable: no adapter registered for canonical target "herdr"`, `retryable=false`;
- zero AWS login pane started for that instruction.

The worker remained healthy and claimed the instruction. This was not worker death.

Current source preserves two disconnected registry paths:

1. managed `serve` builds a registry only from selected-world `host` and `local-tool` consumers:
   - https://github.com/roccho-dev/hq/blob/24ce4e8997875c60122217374137e917404ea203/internal/workerservice/provenance.go#L22-L43
2. the separate one-shot runtime registry registers dedicated `herdr`, `codex`, and `claude` adapters:
   - https://github.com/roccho-dev/hq/blob/24ce4e8997875c60122217374137e917404ea203/cmd/hq-worker/runtime_registry.go#L11-L21

Therefore upgrading only to current `proposals` does not close the gap.

A second physical constraint is observed:

- the generic local-tool executor intentionally starts with an empty environment;
- the installed Windows Herdr CLI cannot connect to its socket with an empty environment;
- bounded `APPDATA` is sufficient for the current installed product;
- the verified executable binding currently carries path/digest/deployment identity, not an accepted Herdr environment contract.

Do not solve this with ambient environment inheritance, PATH, filesystem discovery, or a provider-specific worker branch. The parent checkpoint must fix the smallest binding-owned input required by the exact installed Herdr interface.

## Existing work to reuse; no duplicate implementation

| Existing item | Completed contribution | Boundary retained here |
|---|---|---|
| `hq#99` | Q3 worker, adapter port/registry, safety, result/session evidence | live-provider Q4/Q5 operations were outside closure |
| hq PR `#105` | typed Herdr/Codex/Claude adapter and parser compatibility evidence | dedicated adapters are migration fixtures, not the final production type model |
| `hq#118` / PR `#119` | finite `hq.local-tool.v1`, exact binding preparation, bounded direct execution, managed-worker integration, real `herdr --version` proof | complete Herdr action parity and run views were explicitly excluded |
| `adrs#217` / `envs#34` / PRs `#35/#36` | append-only release compilation, immutable placement, atomic activation | reuse; no second world producer or mutable runtime JSONL |
| envs PR `#40` | verified executable binding ledger and projection | reuse exact binding; open an envs child only after a concrete accepted gap |
| `adrs#220` | finite local/SSH/OCI Vim UX | no generic location target or transport enters this child |
| `adrs#222` / hq PR `#143` | non-interactive verified-resource argv invocation | does not satisfy Herdr-hosted interactive execution |
| `hq#115` | explicit-location real-Vim managed-agent E2E | do not expand its two-task scope into generic Herdr migration |
| `adrs#226` / `envs#50` / PR `#51` | visible Herdr ownership of the Windows hq-worker process | process supervision only; no execution meaning moves to envs |

## Parent checkpoint required before implementation

The `adrs#211` direction is fixed. Only these wire-level choices must be recorded before code:

1. the minimum lifecycle representation for finite Herdr `start`, `read`, `wait/observe`, and reconstruction actions;
2. the terminal condition for successful Herdr start: confirmed handoff/native reference versus child-process terminal completion;
3. bounded binding-owned environment or explicit provider input needed to identify the installed Herdr socket/configuration, with no ambient inheritance;
4. structured native process/session/pane references and validation rules;
5. the compatibility period and removal gate for dedicated `target=herdr`;
6. exact negative behavior when Herdr is unavailable, the binding is stale, provider input is absent, or a native reference is malformed.

Do not reopen the rejected generic runner, recursive execution DSL, generic PTY, generic SSH, generic OCI, shell string, or provider-specific Vim alternatives.

## Bounded implementation scope

After the parent checkpoint:

1. extend or reuse the strict finite local-tool wire only as required by the accepted Herdr lifecycle contract;
2. load Herdr tool/action definitions only from the immutable selected world;
3. resolve executable and bounded provider input only through the exact selected profile/binding;
4. execute only separated argv through the generic bounded process boundary;
5. lower production Herdr commands to generic `local-tool`, not a managed-worker `herdr` registration;
6. preserve dedicated adapters only as compatibility/parity fixtures until required actions and native references pass;
7. add source guards proving no new provider-named production branch enters generic worker or Vim code;
8. return final evidence and exact identities to `adrs#211` without closing that parent prematurely.

## Required physical proof

```text
running Herdr
  -> native Vim + edits-owned client
  -> completion from selected immutable world
  -> one explicit HqSubmit
  -> exactly one accepted instruction
  -> same already-running managed hq-worker
  -> generic local-tool preparation
  -> exact verified Herdr executable
  -> visible named Herdr pane
  -> exact WSLC/OCI AWS login argv, no shell
  -> canonical result.v1
  -> validated native process/pane references
```

Prove:

- completion and selection append zero rows;
- submit appends exactly one instruction;
- no `adapter_unavailable` occurs;
- no manual worker restart or per-submit registry environment is required;
- the pane is visible and accepts the intended interactive provider flow;
- result and native references remain readable after Vim restart;
- read/observe/reconstruct starts zero duplicate AWS/OCI processes;
- a second finite Herdr action can be added through world/release plus binding/profile data only.

Destructive gates:

- missing, stale, mismatched, or tampered Herdr binding starts zero child processes;
- missing bounded provider input starts zero child processes;
- ambient environment cannot silently select another Herdr server;
- malformed or missing native references are typed non-green;
- unknown tool/action or wrong version starts zero processes;
- PATH, shell, cwd, registry, filesystem, or recent-session discovery remains absent;
- duplicate consumption starts zero duplicate Herdr/OCI processes;
- worker restart does not replay after an ambiguous post-effect boundary;
- existing direct one-shot local-tool, `host.open`, and verified-resource paths remain green;
- closing or restarting Vim does not terminate the Herdr-owned interactive process.

## Non-goals

- generic PTY/TUI execution or arbitrary leased stdin;
- generic SSH or OCI target;
- recursive provider/wrapper graph;
- shell command strings or shell evaluation;
- migrating `host.open` merely for taxonomy uniformity;
- worker process supervision or boot composition;
- making Herdr queue, scheduler, run, retry, history, or result authority;
- deleting dedicated adapters before exact parity proof;
- changing submit or approval authority;
- closing `adrs#211`, `adrs#220`, `adrs#222`, or `hq#115` through this child.

## Done

Close this child only when:

1. the exact parent checkpoint is linked;
2. the implementation PR is merged on the then-current `proposals` base;
3. Linux and Windows tests prove the generic boundary and applicable destructive cases;
4. physical real-Vim `oci.aws.login.herdr` succeeds through the same managed worker;
5. native Herdr process/pane evidence and canonical result evidence share exact instruction/run identity;
6. another finite Herdr action changes data/binding only and zero generic hq/Vim code;
7. dedicated adapter retention or deletion is stated from exact parity evidence;
8. final readback is appended to `adrs#211` and states what remains outstanding there."}

